## Token signing

Membership `v2.8.0` requires a PKCS#1 RSA private key. Configure
`config.auth.signingKey` with either PEM text in `value` or the name of an
existing Kubernetes Secret in `existingSecret`. Helm refuses to render when
both are empty. `existingSecret` takes precedence over `value`; `key` defaults
to `signing.key` and must not be empty when using a Secret.

```yaml
config:
  auth:
    signingKey:
      value: ""
      existingSecret: membership-signing-key
      key: signing.key
```

The Secret must exist in the release namespace and contain the PEM text at
the selected key. Membership receives it through `SIGNING_KEY`. To supply a
local PEM file instead, pass `--set-file config.auth.signingKey.value=/path/to/key.pem`.
Keep private keys outside version control. The chart validates configuration
presence; Membership validates the PEM format and rejects the compromised legacy key.

Generate a new key with OpenSSL 3:

```sh
umask 077
openssl genrsa -traditional -out membership-signing-key.pem 4096
```

### Upgrade to Membership v2.8.0

Before upgrading, provision a new key and configure the values above. Apply the
`bind-access-tokens-to-signed-jwt` migration before replacing Membership.
Existing access tokens must be issued again and downstream validators must
refresh their JWKS caches. Follow the
[Membership signing and rotation guide](https://github.com/formancehq/membership-api/blob/v2.8.0/docs/token-signing.md)
for the rollout order. Rolling back the chart does not restore old tokens.

## Migration

### From v0.38 To v1.0.0

#### EE Licence

Membership now need a EE licence. You can get a licence from the Formance team. The licence is valid for 1 cluster.
Then configure it through the `global.licence.token` and `global.licence.clusterID` values. See [Licence configuration](#licence-configuration) for more information.

#### RBAC

Membership service contains a behavior-breaking change within the RBAC module.

Before, permissions were managed dynamically on the organization and stack with a *fallback* on the organization resource. (default organization accesses and default stack accesses). Which led to a lot of confusion and inconsistency regarding the user's permissions

The fallback has been removed from the RBAC module and is only used when a new user joins the organization.

#### Breaking changes

Membership chart now use `.global.platform.<service>.oauth.client` to generate a client and allow the ability to integrate with another chart. specific configuration can added through `.config.auth.additionalOAuthClients` value.

